  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1214 AM EST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nA surface cold front is located across KY, along a dewpoint gradient \nand near the back edge of overcast clouds. No precip is observed \nwith the front and the upper trough. The presence of clouds ahead of \nthis front should prevent fog development. Clouds will persist \nthrough the early morning, then clear out in the mid to late morning \nonce the front moves east and drier air builds in. High temperatures \nwill be cooler today, in the upper 50s to lower 60s in most spots. \n\nBroad troughing along the East Coast will continue through the \nweekend, then the pattern aloft becomes more zonal for Monday. This \nwill maintain dry weather with a slight warming trend for the first \nhalf of next week. A split flow develops, with a southern stream \ntrough ejecting out of northern Mexico and Texas. This will likely \njust bring some cloud cover to our area on Tuesday, as moisture \nappears limited. \n\nA pattern shift comes during the second half of next week, and the \nmodels have been consistent with this shift for the past several \nruns. A deep upper trough drops out of central Canada and evolves \ninto a closed low over the Great Lakes, with the northern jet stream \ndropping into the Deep South. Several spokes of energy rotate around \nthis closed low, which may bring multiple reinforcing shots of cold \nair from Canada from Thursday through next weekend, along with \nprecip chances. We could be looking at temperatures well below \nnormal late next week, and potentially the first meaningful snowfall \nof the season for some spots in the southern Appalachians. This will \nbear watching over the next several days.",
